<!doctype html><html lang="en-GB"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Document editing · Umicom Framework</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../assets/site.css"><script defer src="../assets/site.js"></script>@UMICOM_SITE_ICONS@</head><body><header class="top">@UMICOM_SITE_BRAND@<nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="index.html">Wiki</a><a href="../source-map.html">Source map</a></nav></header><main id="content"><h1>Editing source files in Umicom Studio</h1>
<p>You are updating Umicom Notes. The programme currently displays three saved notes, and you want it to display five. That small change is a useful way to practise selecting text, copying it, reversing an edit and saving the result.</p>
<p>An <strong>editor draft</strong> is the text you are working on in a tab. The <strong>saved file</strong> is the version stored on disk. Editing, Cut, Paste, Undo and Redo change the draft. Save is a separate operation that writes it to the file.</p>
<nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents"><a href="#practice">Practise the commands</a><a href="#paste">When Paste takes time</a><a href="#contracts">Use the Framework contracts</a><a href="#example">Complete example</a><a href="#source">Find the source</a><a href="#tests">Check the result</a></nav>
<h2 id="practice">1. Practise with a Notes project</h2>
<p>Create a disposable C23 Console Application with <strong>File → New Project</strong>, or open an existing practice project through <strong>Open Workspace Folder</strong>. Give it the application name <strong>Umicom Notes</strong> and open <code>src/main.c</code>. Do not replace an unrelated working project.</p>
<p>Enter the following programme and save it. The variable <code>savedNotes</code> holds an unsigned whole number. <code>%u</code> tells <code>printf</code> how to display that number, and <code>\n</code> starts a new output line.</p><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void)
{
    unsigned savedNotes = 3U;
    printf(&quot;Umicom Notes: %u saved notes\n&quot;, savedNotes);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Click inside the source editor before using the main Edit menu. Its commands operate on the active source document. Shortcuts used inside other text fields, such as a search box, retain those fields' normal editing behaviour.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it changes</th><th>Try it in Notes</th></tr><tr><td>Undo · Ctrl+Z</td><td>Returns to an earlier draft in the same document.</td><td>Change <code>3U</code> to <code>5U</code>, then Undo. The source should show <code>3U</code> again.</td></tr><tr><td>Redo · Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z</td><td>Reapplies the undone draft change.</td><td>Redo the edit and check that <code>5U</code> returns.</td></tr><tr><td>Copy · Ctrl+C</td><td>Copies selected text; leaves the draft unchanged.</td><td>Select <code>savedNotes</code> and Copy it.</td></tr><tr><td>Cut · Ctrl+X</td><td>Copies selected text, then removes it from the draft.</td><td>Cut the selected variable name, then Undo to restore it.</td></tr><tr><td>Paste · Ctrl+V</td><td>Replaces the selected text, or inserts at the caret.</td><td>Paste into a deliberate selection, then inspect the result.</td></tr><tr><td>Select All · Ctrl+A</td><td>Selects the complete supported source document.</td><td>Select All, then click beside the variable to clear the selection.</td></tr><tr><td>Delete Selection</td><td>Removes the selection without replacing clipboard content.</td><td>Delete a selected comment, then Undo it.</td></tr></table></div><p>The <strong>caret</strong> is the insertion point. A selection is a highlighted range. Copy and Cut need a nonempty selection; Delete Selection does nothing when no text is selected. Read-only source permits Copy and Select All but refuses changes and history traversal. A document can become read-only after a menu was opened, so the operation checks again when it runs.</p>
<p>Undo and Redo use the same document history as the editor bar. A new edit after Undo begins a new history path and removes the old Redo path. History is bounded to 32 entries and 16 MiB per stack; older entries are discarded as those limits are reached. Closing the document discards its in-memory history. Keep important versions in saved files and Git.</p>
<h2 id="paste">2. Understand a delayed Paste</h2>
<p>The clipboard is shared with other applications. Its text may arrive after you request it. Umicom captures the destination document, its draft and the selected range before asking for the clipboard. It does not wait and then choose whichever tab happens to be active.</p>
<p>Switching to a second tab does not redirect the incoming text. Provided the original destination is still unchanged, Paste edits that original draft and leaves the second tab active. If you change the original draft, its saved revision, path, caret or selection before the text arrives, Umicom refuses the old request. Select the intended destination and Paste again. Closing the target or detaching the window also prevents a late reply from changing another document.</p>
<p>One clipboard read can be pending for each editor binding. Further Paste requests report Busy until it finishes. Unavailable clipboard text and unsupported input leave the draft unchanged. Plain UTF-8 text is supported, up to the document's 8 MiB limit. Images, file drops and rich-text formatting are not inserted by this command.</p>
<p>GTK receives the clipboard data before the document limit can be checked. This limit prevents an oversized edit, but it is not a bound on GTK's temporary transfer allocation. Clipboard contents are not automatically added to application logs.</p>
<h2>3. Save and build the intended version</h2>
<p>Return to <code>src/main.c</code>, confirm it contains <code>5U</code> and Save. Then build and run the Notes project. Its output should read <code>Umicom Notes: 5 saved notes</code>. Undo without another Save changes only the open draft; the compiler still reads the saved file.</p>
<p>Keep a second document open with an unsaved comment while practising. Check that editing the source file never saves, replaces or discards that other draft. Use <strong>Report problem</strong> to collect a short interaction log and a screenshot when a control does not behave as expected. State which tab was active, what was selected, the exact action and the resulting file or draft.</p>
<h2 id="contracts">4. Use the same editing contracts in another application</h2>
<p>A <strong>contract</strong> describes what a function accepts, what it returns and who owns its data. The document coordinator is the single owner of edits, history and saved-file operations. A graphical adapter translates a menu choice or keyboard action into a call to that owner; it does not maintain a second document store.</p>
<p>All calls below run on the document owner's thread. A thread is one execution path through the programme. These calls do not make a background worker or lock the filesystem. GTK clipboard completion returns to the GTK thread before applying the captured edit.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Function or type</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Example use</th></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentEditCommandFromId</code></td><td>Recognises the existing <code>edit.*</code> identifiers. Unknown names return Not Found without changing the output.</td><td>The example maps <code>edit.paste</code> to <code>UMI_DOCUMENT_EDIT_PASTE</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentEditState</code> and <code>UmiDocumentCoordinatorGetEditState</code></td><td>Return copied text/selection lengths and read-only/Undo/Redo availability without selecting or saving.</td><td>The example checks that Select All selected the entire source.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentCoordinatorSelectAll</code></td><td>Selects a document by numeric identity; does not activate another tab.</td><td>The example first selects all, then narrows its selection to the digit.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentCoordinatorPrepareEdit</code></td><td>Captures a complete owned draft, its target and revisions. Preparation changes nothing.</td><td>Prepare before starting a delayed clipboard request.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentEditPlanSelection</code></td><td>Returns a borrowed slice and explicit byte length. The slice may not end in a zero byte.</td><td>Check the selected digit using its length; never free this pointer.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentCoordinatorApplyEdit</code></td><td>Checks the capture, then performs one replacement through existing history/store/view operations. Success consumes the plan.</td><td>The example supplies <code>"5"</code> with length 1; an empty replacement deletes the selected range.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentEditPlanDestroy</code></td><td>Releases the captured text and plan. It does not access the borrowed coordinator.</td><td>Release on success, rejection and cancellation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentCoordinatorUndo</code> and <code>UmiDocumentCoordinatorRedo</code></td><td>Traverse the intended document history without switching the active tab.</td><td>The example undoes and redoes the source change while a second draft is active.</td></tr><tr><td><code>UmiDocumentCoordinatorSyncDocument</code></td><td>Records an accepted group of draft edits in the existing store/history without writing to disk.</td><td>The native editor calls it at the end of a user action; the example records its second draft.</td></tr></table></div><h3>Count bytes carefully</h3>
<p>The letter <code>A</code> occupies one UTF-8 byte, but <code>£</code> occupies two and some symbols occupy four. The public selection offsets count bytes. GTK character offsets must be converted before they are placed in the view model. Selection endpoints must not divide a multi-byte character. The coordinator refuses malformed UTF-8 and embedded zero bytes instead of silently altering the replacement.</p>
<h3>Handle failure without losing the draft</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Result</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Next step</th></tr><tr><td>OK</td><td>The draft operation completed. For the native Paste entry point, the immediate OK means only that a clipboard request was submitted.</td><td>Inspect the completion callback for the final Paste result; Save separately.</td></tr><tr><td>Invalid State</td><td>The capture no longer matches the destination, or its selection is invalid.</td><td>Leave the draft intact and prepare a fresh operation.</td></tr><tr><td>Not Found</td><td>The document was closed or no applicable history/selection exists.</td><td>Choose an open document or another available operation.</td></tr><tr><td>Permission Denied</td><td>The source is read-only.</td><td>Copy is still possible; do not force a write.</td></tr><tr><td>Capacity Exceeded</td><td>The replacement would exceed the supported size.</td><td>Reduce the replacement or use a suitable supported document.</td></tr><tr><td>Out of Memory</td><td>A required allocation could not be completed.</td><td>Retain the draft and retry when resources are available.</td></tr><tr><td>Busy or Unavailable</td><td>A paste is already pending, or native text is not available.</td><td>Wait for completion or copy suitable plain text and retry.</td></tr></table></div><h3>Connect a GTK application</h3>
<p><code>UmiGtk4AdapterBindDocumentEditing</code> borrows an existing coordinator and a completion callback. Call it after the adapter window has been prepared. Use <code>UmiGtk4AdapterDocumentCommandEnabled</code> to display availability, and <code>UmiGtk4AdapterDocumentCommand</code> to execute the chosen identifier. Keep the coordinator and callback context alive until the binding is removed.</p><pre><code>/* adapter and documents are already created and remain owned by the host. */
UmiStatus status = UmiGtk4AdapterBindDocumentEditing(adapter, documents,
    OnEditingCompleted, applicationContext);
if (status == UMI_STATUS_OK &amp;&amp;
    UmiGtk4AdapterDocumentCommandEnabled(adapter, &quot;edit.paste&quot;)) {
    status = UmiGtk4AdapterDocumentCommand(adapter, &quot;edit.paste&quot;);
}
/* Before destroying documents or applicationContext: */
(void)UmiGtk4AdapterBindDocumentEditing(adapter, NULL, NULL, NULL);</code></pre><p>This fragment belongs inside an existing host. The native test fixture supplies complete adapter/window setup and cleanup. Rebinding or window destruction cancels and invalidates outstanding reads. Native source-editor clipboard signals and Ctrl+Z/Ctrl+Y/Ctrl+Shift+Z use the same owner. Other entry fields are not replaced by the source-editor binding.</p>
<p>These commands cover the main Edit menu, editor-bar history buttons and standard source-editor shortcuts. Drag-and-drop, primary-selection/middle-button paste and toolkit context-menu enablement are separate interaction paths. Test them separately rather than assuming that one successful clipboard test covers them all.</p>
<h2 id="example">5. Build the complete Notes exercise</h2>
<p>The programme below uses the actual Framework document store, view model and coordinator. It creates its own temporary file, captures a selection, changes tabs, applies the edit to the original target and verifies Undo, Redo and an explicit Save. It supplies the delayed replacement directly so it can run from a terminal without reading your system clipboard.</p>
<h3>Windows</h3><pre><code>Set-Location &quot;C:\umicom\Umicom-Applications&quot;
$env:Path = &quot;C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin;$env:Path&quot;
&amp; &quot;C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin\cmake.exe&quot; --build --preset windows-ucrt64-all-debug --target umicom-document-editing-example --parallel 2
&amp; &quot;.\build\windows-ucrt64-all-debug\bin\umicom-document-editing-example.exe&quot;</code></pre><h3>Linux</h3><pre><code>cd &quot;$HOME/umicom/Umicom-Applications&quot;
cmake --build --preset headless-debug --target umicom-document-editing-example --parallel 2
./build/headless-debug/bin/umicom-document-editing-example</code></pre><p>Use an already configured Applications checkout. The build guide explains configuration. A nonzero exit code means a check failed. The expected output is:</p><pre><code>The source changed from 3 to 5. The second draft stayed active and unchanged.
Undo and Redo used the same document history.
Explicit Save wrote the expected source; reading it back confirmed the result.</code></pre><h3>The complete source</h3><p>File: <code>framework/examples/editor_workflow/edit_commands.c</code></p><pre><code>@UMICOM_SITE_EDIT_COMMANDS_EXAMPLE@</code></pre>
<h3>Follow the programme in order</h3>
<p>The first calls create the command registry, workbench, document store and coordinator. The file is opened through the existing local provider. Select All demonstrates the copied edit-state query, and the next update narrows the selection to the digit.</p>
<p>PrepareEdit creates a private capture. The selected pointer is borrowed from that capture; its explicit length prevents an accidental read past the selection. Opening a second draft models the delay between a clipboard request and its reply. ApplyEdit uses the captured numeric document identity, not the active tab.</p>
<p>The exercise checks both drafts and the active selection before traversing history. It then reads the file to confirm that editing has not saved anything. Only the explicit SaveAs call writes the intended file. A final read checks the bytes that were actually saved. Cleanup releases the plan before its owners and removes only the temporary directory created by this run.</p>
<h2 id="source">6. Find the implementation</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Framework-relative location</th><th>Purpose and example connection</th></tr><tr><td><code>include/umicom/document/edit.h</code></td><td>Public edit state, command identities, captured replacement and targeted history contracts. Every operation is used by the complete example or the test fixture.</td></tr><tr><td><code>src/document/coordinator.c</code></td><td>Validates captured identities and selections, uses the existing commit/history path and keeps UTF-8 history positions on character boundaries. Its private helpers are exercised through the public calls.</td></tr><tr><td><code>include/umicom/ui/gtk4/document_commands.h</code></td><td>Native binding, availability and completion contracts. The GTK fixture demonstrates setup, request and teardown.</td></tr><tr><td><code>adapters/gtk4/document_commands_gtk4.c</code></td><td>Handles clipboard ownership, request lifetime and command dispatch. Cut deletes only after clipboard publication succeeds and the target is still valid.</td></tr><tr><td><code>adapters/gtk4/editor_gtk4.c</code></td><td>Connects source-editor typing groups, clipboard signals and history shortcuts to the shared owner.</td></tr><tr><td><code>adapters/gtk4/gtk4_internal.h</code> and <code>gtk4_adapter.c</code></td><td>Private binding storage and cleanup. No new application-owned document service is created.</td></tr><tr><td><code>src/application_shell/profiles/edit_menu.c</code></td><td>Keeps the shared menu contribution catalogue and adds Select All/Delete Selection.</td></tr><tr><td><code>tests/document_editing/</code></td><td>Core, allocation-failure and native clipboard tests. Read a case beside the contract it verifies.</td></tr><tr><td><code>examples/editor_workflow/edit_commands.c</code></td><td>The complete runnable exercise above. Its CMake target also supports an installed SDK.</td></tr></table></div><p>Studio adopts the binding in <code>applications/studio/src/gui/workbench/runtime/runtime_attach.inc</code> and releases it in <code>runtime_framework.inc</code>. <code>runtime_helpers.inc</code> queries availability; <code>interaction/command_router.inc</code> routes the menu identifiers; <code>interaction/editor_controls.inc</code> uses the same history commands. These include fragments are compiled through the existing Studio workbench translation unit, not as independent programmes.</p>
<h2 id="tests">7. Check the operation, not just the click</h2>
<p>Repeat the Notes exercise through the Edit menu and the source-editor shortcuts. Check the entire draft, the saved file and the second document. Try a read-only document, an empty selection and a new edit after Undo. A build should use the version you explicitly saved.</p>
<p>The core tests can supply a delayed replacement deterministically. They prepare a capture, change the destination and then attempt completion. The native tests use real GTK controls and the system clipboard, so run them in a disposable desktop test session. They use toolkit signals, not operating-system mouse injection. An external test timeout is still needed if a native callback stops responding.</p>
<p>To understand a failure, retain the test output and record the starting document, selected range, exact action and expected result. Never include private clipboard text in a defect report unless it is necessary and you have permission to share it.</p>
<h3>Further reading</h3>
<p>The GTK documentation explains <a href="https://docs.gtk.org/gdk4/method.Clipboard.read_text_async.html">asynchronous clipboard requests</a>, <a href="https://docs.gtk.org/gdk4/method.Clipboard.read_text_finish.html">ownership of returned text</a> and <a href="https://docs.gtk.org/gtk4/class.TextView.html">TextView actions and shortcuts</a>.</p></main><footer>Umicom Foundation</footer></body></html>